1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,300
They have the sharpest teeth known to man, but only bite on command.

2
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:08,600
They brought down the forests and built up the muscle.

3
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:11,900
Though decked out in diamonds, they do our dirty work.

4
00:00:12,300 --> 00:00:16,400
They're a cut above for construction, demolition and even sports.

5
00:00:16,401 --> 00:00:18,100
One, two, go.

6
00:00:19,400 --> 00:00:23,700
Now, let's sink our teeth into Saws on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:39,660
It's an 800-pound behemoth, and it's loose in the Washington State Forest.

8
00:00:41,740 --> 00:00:46,800
Spinning at a blinding 200 miles per hour, it's taking down everything in its path.

9
00:00:53,160 --> 00:00:59,380
It's the circular saw blade on the John Deere 959J, and it means business.

10
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,180
This blade, which at close range sounds more like a jet engine than a song,

11
00:01:06,380 --> 00:01:09,480
takes one second to blow through an 18-inch diameter tree.

12
00:01:10,780 --> 00:01:13,640
Its carbide teeth are bolted to a huge circular blade

13
00:01:13,641 --> 00:01:16,480
that dishes out punishment for up to 10,000 hours,

14
00:01:18,080 --> 00:01:22,560
cutting down as many as 2 million trees before workers have to swap it out.

15
00:01:24,040 --> 00:01:29,480
It's a hardened steel blade that is balanced during the manufacturing process,

16
00:01:29,481 --> 00:01:33,380
much like the flywheel on an engine or the tires on your car,

17
00:01:33,520 --> 00:01:36,360
so that it spins freely and spins balanced.

18
00:01:38,080 --> 00:01:44,320
Spinning at 1,200 RPM, this huge saw blade severs a tree completely before the grab arms clasp it.

19
00:01:45,420 --> 00:01:48,200
If you actually clamp onto a tree and cut it,

20
00:01:48,700 --> 00:01:52,700
you can induce some stress into the tree and actually cause some damage to the tree.

21
00:01:53,260 --> 00:01:57,860
And as a result, you're never clamping onto the tree until after it's cut.

22
00:01:57,861 --> 00:02:03,320
As soon as you see the first chip flying, you have to be closing your grab arms instantly.

23
00:02:04,100 --> 00:02:06,900
Otherwise, you're dropping trees where you don't want them.

24
00:02:08,740 --> 00:02:14,000
The whole rig, the 959J, is commonly known as a feller buncher.

25
00:02:15,200 --> 00:02:22,320
Its specs? 90,000 pounds, 294 horsepower, hydraulics that tilt the cap,

26
00:02:22,321 --> 00:02:27,940
and an 8,700-pound cutting head that rotates 310 degrees.

27
00:02:29,780 --> 00:02:32,980
All this versatility and power is at the service of one thing,

28
00:02:33,640 --> 00:02:37,640
finding a way to level that blade into fresh wood cleanly every time.

29
00:02:40,140 --> 00:02:42,740
There's more to cutting than just speed and sharpness.

30
00:02:43,920 --> 00:02:47,120
Designers have made the saw's teeth wider than the body of the blade.

31
00:02:47,660 --> 00:02:53,220
As they cut, they clear a kerf, a channel that allows the blade to pass without friction and binding.

32
00:02:54,080 --> 00:02:58,740
Creating a kerf, or clearance for the blade, is critical for the performance of any saw.

33
00:03:01,580 --> 00:03:05,840
A skilled feller buncher operator like Dale Stokin is a multitasker.

34
00:03:07,320 --> 00:03:12,820
Maneuvering and stabilizing his machine on steep, slippery ground as he hues down trees,

35
00:03:12,821 --> 00:03:16,840
threads them through the canopy's obstacle course

36
00:03:16,841 --> 00:03:19,880
and piles them like so many matchsticks.

37
00:03:21,320 --> 00:03:24,180
In this $600,000 feller buncher,

38
00:03:24,940 --> 00:03:28,540
Dale can cut down anywhere from 100 to 200 trees an hour.

39
00:03:29,180 --> 00:03:32,840
But how do you stay safe while you're harvesting trees as tall as 20-story buildings?

40
00:03:34,260 --> 00:03:37,180
These polycarbonate windows are literally bulletproof,

41
00:03:37,181 --> 00:03:40,920
and so they're meant to withstand the impact from debris

42
00:03:40,921 --> 00:03:44,060
and trees that may come down out here in the woods.

43
00:03:45,020 --> 00:03:47,380
I haven't had any trees fall on this machine yet.

44
00:03:47,880 --> 00:03:51,960
Tops, rotten pieces, snags, a lot of dead wood in the stand of timber.

45
00:03:52,220 --> 00:03:56,400
They bang on it, but it's built pretty heavy duty.

46
00:03:57,620 --> 00:04:00,600
Ultimately, the key to Dale's safety is knowing his limits.

47
00:04:01,560 --> 00:04:05,380
A lot of times we have timber that's too big for the buncher that can't handle it,

48
00:04:05,381 --> 00:04:09,400
and, you know, the steeper ground, like on the backside here,

49
00:04:09,401 --> 00:04:11,520
he's not going to be able to get in here with that machine.

50
00:04:11,640 --> 00:04:12,860
He slides around too much.

51
00:04:13,660 --> 00:04:16,300
So Dale leaves the job to the crew on foot

52
00:04:16,301 --> 00:04:19,440
to cut down the trees with an entirely different kind of saw,

53
00:04:20,080 --> 00:04:22,260
a steel MS-460 chainsaw.

54
00:04:23,380 --> 00:04:27,180
This chainsaw's motor engages a sprocket that drives the cutting chain.

55
00:04:28,760 --> 00:04:32,120
The saw chain is comprised of alternating left and right cutters.

56
00:04:32,920 --> 00:04:35,740
Each tooth is capable of cleanly slicing off a chip.

57
00:04:35,741 --> 00:04:40,240
But if the cut is incomplete, the opposing cutter will finish the job.

58
00:04:41,300 --> 00:04:45,740
Severed chips are carried away through the chip channel to be ejected at the end of the cut.

59
00:04:50,800 --> 00:04:57,900
With chainsaw in hand, a skilled logger will take down a tree, like this western hemlock, in about 30 seconds.

60
00:04:58,440 --> 00:05:02,540
You start with what's called your top cut, and you start your saw here.

61
00:05:02,541 --> 00:05:08,780
When you're finished with your top cut, you get behind your saw and you look down the

62
00:05:08,781 --> 00:05:14,460
site of your saw out there 50 to 60 feet and sight in on something out there and that will

63
00:05:14,461 --> 00:05:16,420
determine where your tree is going to lay.

64
00:05:16,421 --> 00:05:21,920
Then as your slope cut, you can come up underneath and you got to match up your slope cut with

65
00:05:21,921 --> 00:05:24,660
your top cut perfectly.

66
00:05:24,661 --> 00:05:28,820
The last cut, the back cut, actually fells the tree.

67
00:05:28,821 --> 00:05:35,700
You want to be an inch to two inches above your original undercut and with an even hinge

68
00:05:35,701 --> 00:05:36,700
wood.

69
00:05:36,701 --> 00:05:40,740
Hinge wood is what's considered the wood that's left, which actually steers the tree in the

70
00:05:40,741 --> 00:05:43,580
direction you're falling it.

71
00:05:43,581 --> 00:05:50,940
If you're falling this tree this way and it brushes another tree or an object out there,

72
00:05:50,941 --> 00:05:55,760
the force of that tree springing can spring back and actually push that tree back on the

73
00:05:55,761 --> 00:05:56,760
stump.

74
00:05:56,761 --> 00:06:00,640
come back 20 to 30 feet back up through the timber before.

75
00:06:00,641 --> 00:06:03,680
If it hits something, it'll come up against that step

76
00:06:03,681 --> 00:06:05,200
that you're going to have back here.

77
00:06:05,201 --> 00:06:09,060
And that step of wood will hold that tree upon the stump.

78
00:06:09,061 --> 00:06:12,320
Everything about this job, from falling timber to the saws,

79
00:06:12,321 --> 00:06:14,040
is dangerous.

80
00:06:14,041 --> 00:06:16,380
We had a guy get hurt a few years ago.

81
00:06:16,381 --> 00:06:19,880
He didn't get cut with the saw, but he got smashed by a top,

82
00:06:19,881 --> 00:06:24,200
came out of a tree, and smashed him and broke his back.

83
00:06:24,201 --> 00:06:26,560
With all the sound and fury of power saws,

84
00:06:26,561 --> 00:06:29,740
it's easy to miss some of the subtleties of what makes a saw a saw.

85
00:06:30,580 --> 00:06:33,240
For that, it helps to take a look at a simpler saw.

86
00:06:35,980 --> 00:06:39,140
The panel saw, also known as a hand saw.

87
00:06:39,800 --> 00:06:42,380
Of course, the defining feature of a saw is its teeth.

88
00:06:43,660 --> 00:06:48,180
It's got separately formed teeth that cut into the material

89
00:06:48,181 --> 00:06:54,440
and slice the fibers away so that it cuts very, very quickly through the material.

90
00:06:54,441 --> 00:06:59,360
Saw teeth are only sharp on the forward edge, the cutting edge.

91
00:06:59,361 --> 00:07:03,200
They are also raked, inclined in the direction of the cut.

92
00:07:03,201 --> 00:07:09,560
On all western hand saws, the teeth all basically point in the same direction forward.

93
00:07:09,561 --> 00:07:12,760
That's because these saws are designed to cut on the push stroke.

94
00:07:12,761 --> 00:07:17,480
And when you pull the blade back, the saw isn't cutting.

95
00:07:17,481 --> 00:07:19,960
And for every tooth, there is a gullet.

96
00:07:19,961 --> 00:07:26,420
The gullets are the spaces between the teeth, and these spaces are allowed to fill up with

97
00:07:26,421 --> 00:07:28,920
the material that's being cut away. It's called swarf.

98
00:07:28,921 --> 00:07:30,540
The swarf builds up in these little

99
00:07:30,541 --> 00:07:35,000
gullets, and then as soon as the blade exits at the bottom of the board, the swarf just

100
00:07:35,001 --> 00:07:36,840
falls out of the gullets and falls away.

101
00:07:37,500 --> 00:07:43,240
The last key feature is the kerf, the cutting groove, which in the case of a handsaw is

102
00:07:43,241 --> 00:07:44,560
produced by the set of the teeth.

103
00:07:44,561 --> 00:07:53,779
The set means that alternating teeth are actually bent very, very slightly in one direction and then the other, alternating all the way down the length of the saw

104
00:07:53,780 --> 00:07:54,480
blade.

105
00:07:54,600 --> 00:08:02,260
The actual cutting edge creates a wider curve than the thickness of the blade, so the blade has clearance and doesn't stick in the curve.

106
00:08:03,340 --> 00:08:08,820
Despite all these design features, there's still plenty of skill involved in making a clean cut with a handsaw.

107
00:08:08,821 --> 00:08:16,320
Now the whole trick to getting a nice clean cut is starting off with a nick on the edge.

108
00:08:16,321 --> 00:08:21,060
I'm going to use my thumb to keep the saw from moving sideways, and I'm just going to

109
00:08:21,061 --> 00:08:29,060
pull backwards a little bit to create a starting groove for the saw blade.

110
00:08:29,061 --> 00:08:36,140
Now I'm going to bring the angle of my saw up to about 45 degrees, and I'm going to start

111
00:08:36,141 --> 00:08:36,841
sawing.

112
00:08:36,842 --> 00:08:42,720
I'll lift the saw slightly as I go on the reverse stroke so the teeth aren't dragging.

113
00:08:43,200 --> 00:08:46,080
Keep sawing until I get right near the end.

114
00:08:47,160 --> 00:08:51,180
When I get this close, what I'm going to do is I'm going to hold this piece.

115
00:08:52,500 --> 00:08:53,580
I'm going to slow down.

116
00:08:53,680 --> 00:08:58,660
I'm going to saw very gently at the very end to avoid tearing out the corner.

117
00:08:59,580 --> 00:09:00,340
There's my cut.

118
00:09:02,140 --> 00:09:04,500
But not all saws cut on the push stroke.

119
00:09:04,501 --> 00:09:09,220
Japanese saws all cut on the pull stroke.

120
00:09:09,600 --> 00:09:12,540
Japanese saws are very, very thin.

121
00:09:12,980 --> 00:09:18,500
When you have a thinner saw, that means you have to remove less wood to make the cut, so they cut faster.

122
00:09:19,700 --> 00:09:24,000
Western saws have thicker blades, so they won't buckle on the more powerful push stroke.

123
00:09:26,600 --> 00:09:29,420
Japanese pull saws are designed to cut softer woods.

124
00:09:30,160 --> 00:09:35,120
The reason we want to work this low, we want to be able to use our foot to hold the workpiece down

125
00:09:35,121 --> 00:09:38,700
and then use both our arms on the saw to get lots of power to the cut.

126
00:09:39,260 --> 00:09:44,100
Now at the end of the cut, I slow down a little tiny bit, it comes right off.

127
00:09:47,160 --> 00:09:49,900
East or west, modern saws are efficient.

128
00:09:50,800 --> 00:09:58,720
But their most ancient ancestors, prehistoric flint saws circa 8,000 B.C., were thick and jammed in the cutting groove.

129
00:09:59,420 --> 00:10:06,620
Around 4900 BC, Egyptians and Mesopotamians fashioned the first metal saws of relatively

130
00:10:06,621 --> 00:10:08,420
soft metals like copper.

131
00:10:08,421 --> 00:10:12,320
And actually they could only cut very, very soft woods.

132
00:10:12,321 --> 00:10:17,860
Then we basically progressed to a point where we used harder and harder materials, bronze

133
00:10:17,861 --> 00:10:22,320
and bronze alloys that have the ability to hold a little bit of a better edge right up

134
00:10:22,321 --> 00:10:23,320
until we get to iron.

135
00:10:23,321 --> 00:10:29,400
And of course, iron is where we make a major leap into tools that were really capable of

136
00:10:29,401 --> 00:10:31,160
of doing some pretty serious work,

137
00:10:31,161 --> 00:10:35,840
felling trees and cutting up very thick logs.

138
00:10:37,200 --> 00:10:41,440
The saws of the Iron Age, roughly 700 BC to 50 AD,

139
00:10:41,441 --> 00:10:43,100
incorporated two breakthroughs.

140
00:10:44,560 --> 00:10:47,100
Teeth that were raked in the direction of the cut,

141
00:10:47,101 --> 00:10:48,680
likely in Egyptian innovation.

142
00:10:50,120 --> 00:10:52,840
And from the Romans, set teeth,

143
00:10:52,841 --> 00:10:55,300
bent to the left and right that created a kerf.

144
00:10:55,301 --> 00:11:00,920
Ultimately, with widespread use of steel in the 19th century,

145
00:11:01,580 --> 00:11:07,160
giant two-man cross-cut saws replaced axes for the job of cutting down the old-growth forests.

146
00:11:08,960 --> 00:11:12,780
These saws were equipped with large teeth designed to cut in both directions.

147
00:11:14,060 --> 00:11:15,540
It's called a misery whip.

148
00:11:16,720 --> 00:11:21,880
They nicknamed it that because of listening to it whip back and forth through the trees

149
00:11:21,881 --> 00:11:26,000
and pulling it like a whip, and by the end of the day, it was miserable.

150
00:11:26,800 --> 00:11:30,780
They would take an axe, chop an undercut in the front of the tree,

151
00:11:30,860 --> 00:11:32,500
whichever direction they wanted it to go,

152
00:11:33,020 --> 00:11:35,360
and then they would start with the crosscut saw.

153
00:11:38,240 --> 00:11:40,680
They had to be larger than the tree you were cutting

154
00:11:40,681 --> 00:11:42,980
because you had to have room on each side of the tree

155
00:11:42,981 --> 00:11:45,120
to work your saw back and forth on.

156
00:11:45,240 --> 00:11:48,220
So if you had a tree that was 15 feet,

157
00:11:48,221 --> 00:11:53,900
and in reality you had to have a 20 foot or larger misery whip to get through your saw.

158
00:11:53,901 --> 00:11:56,620
So you were pulling a lot of steel through there.

159
00:11:58,540 --> 00:11:59,240
Okay.

160
00:11:59,241 --> 00:11:59,941
Hold that.

161
00:12:02,580 --> 00:12:03,440
A lot of work.

162
00:12:06,140 --> 00:12:08,300
Now that ain't bad, just a lot of pulling.

163
00:12:10,100 --> 00:12:10,800
You ready, Hoss?

164
00:12:10,980 --> 00:12:11,680
Yeah.

165
00:12:18,220 --> 00:12:24,460
Timber!

166
00:12:24,461 --> 00:12:25,161
Timber!

167
00:12:31,100 --> 00:12:36,420
This saw right here is the reason they always said we'll never be able to cut it all.

168
00:12:37,420 --> 00:12:38,940
You never would have with this saw.

169
00:12:41,520 --> 00:12:47,200
Luckily for loggers, if not for the forest environment, the two-man crosscut saw's day has passed.

170
00:12:47,201 --> 00:12:53,980
But for timber sports competitors like David Moses and his wife Annette from Snoqualmie, Washington,

171
00:12:54,980 --> 00:12:58,400
his old school saw presents an opportunity for them to show their skills.

172
00:12:59,160 --> 00:13:00,320
David's father trains them.

173
00:13:01,700 --> 00:13:06,600
Timers ready. Contestants ready. One, two, go.

174
00:13:06,601 --> 00:13:20,601
This is a modified peg and raker, and the way these cutters are, they'll make two cuts

175
00:13:22,360 --> 00:13:27,360
on either side of the wood, which will make a line, and this raker will come through and

176
00:13:27,361 --> 00:13:29,880
push underneath that, and it'll pull out a noodle.

177
00:13:32,120 --> 00:13:35,000
And the longer the noodle, generally the faster the cut is.

178
00:13:36,600 --> 00:13:40,040
But these teeth that make the saw sharp also make it dangerous.

179
00:13:40,860 --> 00:13:44,580
Annette had a painful reminder of this while competing in July 2006.

180
00:13:45,320 --> 00:13:48,480
We were coming back. We were coming off the return to the bottom.

181
00:13:49,780 --> 00:13:52,080
And we went right across my knee.

182
00:13:53,300 --> 00:13:58,000
And somehow it moved. Just came right across. Ended up with 22 stitches.

183
00:13:58,780 --> 00:14:00,800
But it wasn't the end of Annette's competition.

184
00:14:01,800 --> 00:14:05,720
In fact, we kept going and then when we were done, I went to the hospital.

185
00:14:05,721 --> 00:14:07,520
came back and single-bucked.

186
00:14:08,800 --> 00:14:10,080
We're a different breed.

187
00:14:11,220 --> 00:14:13,480
And this chainsaw is also a breed apart.

188
00:14:14,920 --> 00:14:17,920
Few saws are as fast and as dangerous as David's hot saw,

189
00:14:18,220 --> 00:14:21,100
a stock chainsaw that has been modified for maximum power.

190
00:14:22,500 --> 00:14:23,900
This is a hot saw.

191
00:14:23,901 --> 00:14:27,780
It's a Rotex hot saw, which is half a snowmobile,

192
00:14:28,500 --> 00:14:30,060
twin-cylinder snowmobile engine.

193
00:14:30,820 --> 00:14:34,460
This $5,000, 35-horsepower hot saw

194
00:14:35,020 --> 00:14:38,160
runs on racing fuel, has a motorcycle exhaust pipe,

195
00:14:38,860 --> 00:14:40,100
weighs in at 50 pounds,

196
00:14:40,560 --> 00:14:44,600
and feels nothing like a stock chainsaw that would have just one-sixth the power.

197
00:14:47,380 --> 00:14:51,160
There's just a tremendous amount of force, so it takes some getting used to.

198
00:14:51,220 --> 00:14:53,020
So the first year I was a little bit scared of it.

199
00:14:53,940 --> 00:14:55,380
You don't want to stand at the front end of the saw

200
00:14:55,381 --> 00:15:00,060
because there's possibilities of any of the teeth breaking off and shooting out the end,

201
00:15:00,360 --> 00:15:02,200
which can come off like a bullet.

202
00:15:02,201 --> 00:15:05,600
In action, the saw lives up to its powerful billing.

203
00:15:07,180 --> 00:15:12,280
Timers ready. Contestants ready. One, two, go.

204
00:15:22,620 --> 00:15:29,900
8.53, that's with three cuts. So that's with his practice chain. Not bad.

205
00:15:29,901 --> 00:15:35,640
Whether for work or sport, power timber saws are ruthlessly efficient.

206
00:15:36,480 --> 00:15:42,120
And once the logs they fell reach the mill, they're greeted by gangs of super sharp specialty saws.

207
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:46,220
But how do Sawyers keep these blades sharp day in and day out?

208
00:15:46,720 --> 00:15:49,620
Through the obscure crafts of benching and swaging.

209
00:15:50,200 --> 00:15:50,900
That's how.

210
00:15:54,820 --> 00:15:58,140
Timber cutting is one of the most lethal jobs in the United States.

211
00:15:58,141 --> 00:16:06,200
The annual on-the-job death rate for loggers is over 110 per 100,000 workers, or 30 times the national average.

212
00:16:07,040 --> 00:16:09,300
Saw's will return on Modern Marvels.

213
00:16:12,680 --> 00:16:15,880
We now return to Saw's on Modern Marvels.

214
00:16:17,620 --> 00:16:19,180
The modern lumber mill.

215
00:16:20,040 --> 00:16:22,640
Its basic components haven't changed in 100 years.

216
00:16:22,641 --> 00:16:29,020
But the heart and soul of the mill, the saws, are thinner, sharper, stronger, and faster.

217
00:16:31,580 --> 00:16:35,720
The Dahlstrom Lumber Mill in Hoquiam, Washington, is a big log mill.

218
00:16:36,300 --> 00:16:38,560
It handles, well, big logs.

219
00:16:38,920 --> 00:16:42,420
Generally that means a log maybe over 20 inches on the small end.

220
00:16:42,640 --> 00:16:45,660
We can run up to 5 foot from the log through our mill.

221
00:16:46,360 --> 00:16:52,020
As these large western hemlocks roll into the mill, the first saw they face is a deck saw.

222
00:16:52,640 --> 00:16:57,500
The deck saw we have here is a chainsaw, basically like every chainsaw.

223
00:16:57,620 --> 00:16:59,420
This one has bigger teeth on it.

224
00:16:59,660 --> 00:17:00,980
It's got an 8-foot bar on it.

225
00:17:01,300 --> 00:17:03,800
It's capable of cutting logs up to over 5 feet.

226
00:17:04,520 --> 00:17:09,040
The chainsaw bucks the logs, or in layman's terms, cuts them to length.

227
00:17:10,380 --> 00:17:15,140
Then the operator runs them through the debarker, which preps them for the head rig,

228
00:17:15,720 --> 00:17:19,180
a steel bandsaw that does most of the job of sawing logs into lumber.

229
00:17:20,040 --> 00:17:22,620
The band is actually 35 feet in length.

230
00:17:23,460 --> 00:17:28,300
The rim speed, or the speed of the blade, is about 8,800 feet a minute, or 100 miles an hour.

231
00:17:29,300 --> 00:17:34,240
This large band saw turning on six-foot diameter wheels is the workhorse of the mill.

232
00:17:35,020 --> 00:17:37,040
It's large enough to cut the big logs.

233
00:17:37,320 --> 00:17:39,440
It's more energy efficient than a circular saw.

234
00:17:41,020 --> 00:17:45,960
And it slices a thinner kerf than a circular saw, cutting back on wood waste.

235
00:17:45,961 --> 00:17:54,960
As the log approaches, the head sawyer attempts to slice the most high-grade or appearance-grade lumber out of the outside of the log.

236
00:17:56,060 --> 00:17:58,620
A laser helps the sawyer to place the bandsaw.

237
00:18:00,860 --> 00:18:06,460
The cants, sliced boards that have not been edged, roll down the conveyor belt towards the edger.

238
00:18:07,240 --> 00:18:10,840
Every saw in the edger is identified on that table with a laser light,

239
00:18:10,940 --> 00:18:14,980
so the operator knows if he sends that piece of wood in there exactly where it would be cut.

240
00:18:15,960 --> 00:18:24,620
A 250 horsepower motor drives a bank of six coupled, or gang, circular saws, spinning at 1,750 rpm.

241
00:18:25,820 --> 00:18:28,460
The blades are expensive and require constant maintenance.

242
00:18:29,960 --> 00:18:34,220
Friction builds heat, and heat is like the enemy of saw blades or steel.

243
00:18:34,520 --> 00:18:35,800
It warps and distorts it.

244
00:18:36,180 --> 00:18:37,740
You've got to keep your saw blades cool.

245
00:18:38,460 --> 00:18:44,300
Our band mills and our edger are water-cooled as their saw and water is sprayed on the blades to help keep them cool.

246
00:18:45,960 --> 00:18:52,040
In addition, carbide cutting teeth, which are wider than the plate, carve out kerf for the saw to spin.

247
00:18:53,980 --> 00:18:56,000
Friction is also the enemy of the bandsaw.

248
00:18:56,880 --> 00:19:00,040
When you get a bandsaw high, it can possibly leave the wheel.

249
00:19:00,440 --> 00:19:02,740
It distorts it so bad and it can walk off the wheel.

250
00:19:05,060 --> 00:19:12,680
To maintain the bandsaws, a saw filer sharpens and widens the teeth so that they'll cut a broad enough kerf for the blade to run cleanly.

251
00:19:12,681 --> 00:19:17,060
This is called swedging.

252
00:19:17,061 --> 00:19:22,320
It's how we make the saw teeth wider than the plate of the saw.

253
00:19:22,321 --> 00:19:26,780
This turns and it forces the metal up to the point of the tooth, which makes it spread

254
00:19:26,781 --> 00:19:29,960
out and wider than the plate.

255
00:19:29,961 --> 00:19:39,720
And then we shape it back and then grind it sharp and it's ready to go.

256
00:19:39,721 --> 00:19:44,680
With the help of their dog, Marius, John and Laurel Robarge run the blade maintenance shop

257
00:19:44,681 --> 00:19:46,680
at Dahlstrom Lumber.

258
00:19:46,681 --> 00:19:51,000
As they sharpen the blades, over several months they will grind the width of each blade down

259
00:19:51,001 --> 00:19:53,180
by a couple of inches.

260
00:19:53,181 --> 00:20:00,480
This process extends the life of the blades, but eventually makes them too narrow to use.

261
00:20:00,481 --> 00:20:02,320
Heat also causes blades to warp.

262
00:20:02,321 --> 00:20:09,680
You'll get these hot spots and it'll expand and then your saw starts wobbling in there.

263
00:20:09,681 --> 00:20:17,080
and then we get them after that and you just sit in and you bench them until the lumps are out

264
00:20:17,081 --> 00:20:23,860
and the teeth are sharp benching is basically what i'm doing on this anvil um making sure that

265
00:20:23,861 --> 00:20:30,860
if you got high spots that you got to pound them back down despite marius's obvious calm around the

266
00:20:30,861 --> 00:20:37,120
razor sharp blades laurel takes nothing for granted even gold they're dangerous you know

267
00:20:37,121 --> 00:20:42,100
It's just, you just gotta give them the respect they deserve.

268
00:20:43,760 --> 00:20:48,600
As the lumber leaves the mill, it's on its way to becoming an essential part of some kind of structure.

269
00:20:49,820 --> 00:20:52,380
But one day that structure may face remodeling.

270
00:20:54,740 --> 00:21:00,180
When that time comes, an entirely different saw, the Milwaukee Sawzall, will be there.

271
00:21:01,320 --> 00:21:06,800
The Sawzall is a reciprocating saw, a power saw with a blade that moves back and forth during cutting.

272
00:21:07,120 --> 00:21:12,120
The Sawzall's aim is to live up to its name.

273
00:21:12,121 --> 00:21:14,120
What can't you use a Sawzall for?

274
00:21:14,121 --> 00:21:20,120
From cutting birthday cake, to lumber, to demolition, to fire and rescue, to...

275
00:21:20,121 --> 00:21:24,120
I even knew a guy, he talked about, he wanted to get rid of a stump,

276
00:21:24,121 --> 00:21:28,120
so he actually used it to cut down in the ground to cut the roots of the stump,

277
00:21:28,121 --> 00:21:30,120
so he could pull it out.

278
00:21:30,121 --> 00:21:36,120
The Super Sawzall is the top of the Milwaukee line and includes orbital cutting action.

279
00:21:36,121 --> 00:21:39,880
Normally a reciprocating action is straight back and forth.

280
00:21:40,380 --> 00:21:41,960
Orbital cutting is more of a circular cut.

281
00:21:42,020 --> 00:21:43,260
It's a much more aggressive cut.

282
00:21:43,920 --> 00:21:46,640
We have a unique feature to Milwaukee.

283
00:21:46,800 --> 00:21:48,680
It's a 360-degree rotating handle.

284
00:21:49,020 --> 00:21:53,560
This is nice because it allows the contractor to adjust the handle to the most comfortable position.

285
00:21:55,040 --> 00:21:58,320
It generates 0 to 3,000 strokes per minute.

286
00:21:58,720 --> 00:22:01,320
So it's literally the fastest reciprocating saw on the market.

287
00:22:02,080 --> 00:22:06,220
The Sawzall has no problem cutting through wood, screws, and nails.

288
00:22:07,060 --> 00:22:08,620
Even the steel hinges on this door.

289
00:22:09,960 --> 00:22:16,380
The Sawzall was introduced in 1951 and originally developed for plumbers and electricians.

290
00:22:16,500 --> 00:22:24,500
And actually what they were looking for was a compact, durable, lightweight solution to cut basically anything that they ran into.

291
00:22:25,780 --> 00:22:28,980
The original Sawzall was a heavy all-metal tool.

292
00:22:28,981 --> 00:22:35,100
Today's version incorporates plastics and nylons for lighter weight and vibration dampening.

293
00:22:36,600 --> 00:22:43,400
This super sawzall and its blades are made at a Milwaukee electric tool plant, which is not in Milwaukee.

294
00:22:44,480 --> 00:22:49,360
In Greenwood, Mississippi, the heart of the Mississippi Delta, catfish and cotton.

295
00:22:50,860 --> 00:22:53,840
The building itself is 150,000 square feet.

296
00:22:54,840 --> 00:22:57,380
We make tens of thousands of sawzalls a year.

297
00:22:57,381 --> 00:23:04,780
The key to making a machine that saws just about anything is manufacturing blades for all types of substrates.

298
00:23:06,240 --> 00:23:08,300
Tooth size determines the cutting speed.

299
00:23:09,440 --> 00:23:13,940
Smaller teeth cut harder material like metals, but remove less material with each stroke.

300
00:23:14,880 --> 00:23:17,960
And a thinner blade means less substrate will actually have to be cut.

301
00:23:18,600 --> 00:23:21,680
The blade we're looking at now is for metal cutting.

302
00:23:22,080 --> 00:23:24,940
And the reason we know that is because the teeth are finer.

303
00:23:24,941 --> 00:23:33,280
There's more TPI or C for inch because you don't want to have an as aggressive bite when

304
00:23:33,281 --> 00:23:35,280
you're cutting metal.

305
00:23:35,281 --> 00:23:40,180
Comparing it to wood, you can take a heavier cut, a larger chip because the wood is softer.

306
00:23:40,181 --> 00:23:43,180
But on metal, you have to have a finer tooth.

307
00:23:43,181 --> 00:23:48,100
As you see on this blade, the tooth is much different from the previous one.

308
00:23:48,101 --> 00:23:49,100
It's much larger.

309
00:23:49,101 --> 00:23:51,060
The angles are different.

310
00:23:51,061 --> 00:23:52,900
The depth of the gullet is different.

311
00:23:52,901 --> 00:23:59,240
And this one is designed for a very aggressive sheetrock and plaster cutting.

312
00:23:59,241 --> 00:24:02,780
There are no saws involved in the manufacture of saw blades.

313
00:24:02,781 --> 00:24:04,280
The teeth are ground in.

314
00:24:04,281 --> 00:24:11,280
The blade process begins with the uncoiler, with a coil of steel.

315
00:24:11,281 --> 00:24:16,040
The uncoiler uncoils it into the straightener.

316
00:24:16,041 --> 00:24:20,300
The blade material is straightened prior to going into the press.

317
00:24:20,301 --> 00:24:26,920
Once it gets into the press, the blades are stamped out for the configuration of the blade itself.

318
00:24:27,360 --> 00:24:33,440
Once they are loaded off of the press, the blades are accumulated into handfuls for the robot.

319
00:24:34,060 --> 00:24:37,860
The robot swings over, picks it up, and loads it into the grinder.

320
00:24:39,480 --> 00:24:44,240
A ceramic grinding wheel grinds the saw's tooth pattern into a pack of 90 blades.

321
00:24:44,241 --> 00:24:53,020
The grinding wheel forms and sharpens the teeth as a mixture of water and lubricant washes metal filings away.

322
00:24:56,180 --> 00:25:02,420
Workers then load the blades into furnaces to temper the metal in order to retain its hardness and its flexibility.

323
00:25:04,140 --> 00:25:10,160
Sawzall blades will cut pretty much anything, but these blades make just one tough cut all day long.

324
00:25:10,161 --> 00:25:14,720
To saw through stone takes teeth made of the hardest substance of all.

325
00:25:15,280 --> 00:25:17,260
And even then, it's a gritty job.

326
00:25:19,760 --> 00:25:27,200
In addition to cutting lumber, band saws are commonly used in the food processing industry to cut meat, fish, and poultry.

327
00:25:28,340 --> 00:25:30,380
Saws will return on Modern Marvels.

328
00:25:32,620 --> 00:25:35,720
We now return to Saws on Modern Marvels.

329
00:25:35,721 --> 00:25:41,900
The National Cathedral, the Empire State Building, the Pentagon.

330
00:25:42,640 --> 00:25:45,960
They all have one thing in common, Indiana limestone.

331
00:25:46,620 --> 00:25:50,660
What's unique about Indiana limestone is its ability to be worked.

332
00:25:51,160 --> 00:25:54,180
It's a soft stone relative to limestone.

333
00:25:54,181 --> 00:25:55,940
It's very consistent in color.

334
00:25:56,720 --> 00:25:59,240
For that reason, it's been chosen as a building stone.

335
00:26:00,540 --> 00:26:04,120
The stone projects an aura of permanence, stateliness, calm.

336
00:26:04,121 --> 00:26:11,120
But today the process of cutting it into blocks is anything but calm.

337
00:26:11,121 --> 00:26:16,120
At the quarry, the first cut is the deepest.

338
00:26:16,121 --> 00:26:20,120
In this case, 15 feet down into the limestone bed.

339
00:26:20,121 --> 00:26:24,120
The saw for the job? A W.F. Myers diamond belt saw.

340
00:26:24,121 --> 00:26:27,120
Though designed specifically for the task of quarrying stone,

341
00:26:27,121 --> 00:26:31,120
it resembles another popular saw.

342
00:26:31,121 --> 00:26:39,620
Just picture a chainsaw, for example, and we put a belt around a bar that is in geometry

343
00:26:39,621 --> 00:26:43,600
very similar to a handheld chainsaw.

344
00:26:43,601 --> 00:26:47,720
Instead of using a chain, we use a belt that has the diamond segments and the plastic around

345
00:26:47,721 --> 00:26:48,720
it.

346
00:26:48,721 --> 00:26:53,160
The steel teeth of a chainsaw couldn't stand up to limestone, but the belt saw's

347
00:26:53,161 --> 00:26:58,780
diamond-studded teeth will slice out almost 30,000 square feet of limestone.

348
00:26:58,781 --> 00:27:02,020
needing replacement after 375 hours of operation.

349
00:27:04,080 --> 00:27:09,800
The belts cost roughly $200 a foot and can be as much as 42 feet in length.

350
00:27:12,000 --> 00:27:14,020
Cutting with diamonds is a simple principle.

351
00:27:14,700 --> 00:27:16,900
To cut something hard, it takes something harder.

352
00:27:17,540 --> 00:27:19,980
And diamonds are one of the hardest materials known to man.

353
00:27:20,700 --> 00:27:23,160
Diamonds themselves are the cutting tool.

354
00:27:24,080 --> 00:27:25,460
They're just like the teeth on a saw.

355
00:27:25,461 --> 00:27:28,780
powders itself what holds the diamonds in, but the diamonds do the cutting.

356
00:27:29,500 --> 00:27:35,540
The powder, in this case 20% carbide and 80% cobalt, is mixed with the diamonds,

357
00:27:35,900 --> 00:27:39,200
cold-pressed into shape, and then hot-pressed to form a hardened segment.

358
00:27:40,020 --> 00:27:45,760
As these diamond-infused segments cut stone, the cobalt-carbide alloy slowly wears away,

359
00:27:46,280 --> 00:27:48,740
continually exposing fresh diamonds at the cutting surface.

360
00:27:49,540 --> 00:27:53,760
The diamonds sit in through the segment,

361
00:27:54,580 --> 00:27:57,800
and as this segment wears, the diamonds stay sharp

362
00:27:57,801 --> 00:28:01,120
on the surface of the segment all the way through.

363
00:28:01,540 --> 00:28:05,520
The diamond segments are then braised, or soldered at high temperatures,

364
00:28:06,080 --> 00:28:06,880
to steel mounts.

365
00:28:07,540 --> 00:28:12,700
Heated to 1,200 degrees, the molten silver solder fuses the segment to the steel mount.

366
00:28:13,640 --> 00:28:15,040
It gets cherry red.

367
00:28:15,041 --> 00:28:19,240
It has to to melt the silver, and you've got to work it around

368
00:28:19,241 --> 00:28:24,360
and make sure that you have a good surface on each one so that it holds good.

369
00:28:24,800 --> 00:28:28,120
Because as you probably can figure, it takes a pretty good hold

370
00:28:28,121 --> 00:28:33,320
because that stone, whenever it hits that stone, that's what does the cutting of the stone.

371
00:28:34,280 --> 00:28:38,900
The segments will cut everything from limestone to granite to marble to slate.

372
00:28:39,580 --> 00:28:43,400
The segments are sharp, but they wouldn't stay that way without lots of water.

373
00:28:45,040 --> 00:28:47,880
The belt saw guzzles 50 gallons a minute.

374
00:28:48,560 --> 00:28:52,580
It pressurizes the water in a labyrinthine system inside the hollow guide bar

375
00:28:52,581 --> 00:28:55,820
and shoots it out beneath the belt at 50 psi.

376
00:28:56,860 --> 00:29:00,140
The belt hydroplanes on the pressurized water, reducing wear.

377
00:29:00,760 --> 00:29:04,560
It also allows the cuttings that we've created to be flushed out

378
00:29:04,561 --> 00:29:08,060
so that we're not constantly recutting the same old stone

379
00:29:08,061 --> 00:29:10,640
and prematurely wearing out the belt.

380
00:29:10,641 --> 00:29:18,520
The computer-controlled saw will cut a 15-foot-deep, 70-foot-long channel in eight hours.

381
00:29:19,320 --> 00:29:20,200
It's an auto saw.

382
00:29:20,640 --> 00:29:24,940
It'll program it right there in that cut, and it'll go down all on its own automatically.

383
00:29:25,180 --> 00:29:27,080
Never, never have to fool with it.

384
00:29:28,300 --> 00:29:32,680
After the saw has completed the vertical cut, workers drill horizontal holes

385
00:29:32,681 --> 00:29:35,280
and hydraulically split the rock along the bottom,

386
00:29:36,080 --> 00:29:41,160
removing the limestone in 50-foot-long, 5-foot-wide, 15-foot-deep sections.

387
00:29:44,860 --> 00:29:47,560
Though the Myers quarry saw is cutting-edge,

388
00:29:48,060 --> 00:29:51,160
it's merely the most modern application of stone-cutting techniques

389
00:29:51,161 --> 00:29:53,380
first practiced thousands of years ago.

390
00:29:58,060 --> 00:30:01,260
The most prolific ancient stone cutters were the Egyptians,

391
00:30:01,620 --> 00:30:03,520
who worked extensively with limestone.

392
00:30:05,280 --> 00:30:11,200
Metal saws fashioned of copper, bronze, and sometimes iron were used with sand or emery,

393
00:30:11,780 --> 00:30:17,200
a cutting grit like modern-day diamonds, to fashion blocks of limestone over 6,000 years ago.

394
00:30:17,860 --> 00:30:21,700
In order to get the abrasive particles to stick to the edge,

395
00:30:21,701 --> 00:30:25,640
they'd use a resinous substance of some kind, you know, plant resins,

396
00:30:26,040 --> 00:30:31,160
to get those particles against the stone or the material that they were trying to cut.

397
00:30:35,280 --> 00:30:44,920
In stone mills, a modern version of this abrasive-fed cutting technique was widespread before the advent of diamond-studded cutting teeth in the 1960s.

398
00:30:46,420 --> 00:30:49,498
One hundred years ago, gang saws like these at B.G.

399
00:30:49,499 --> 00:30:56,800
Hoadley quarries would have been fed with an abrasive slurry of sand or silica to enable them to grind through limestone.

400
00:30:57,940 --> 00:31:02,760
Today, these old-fashioned saws are still at work, but they've been updated with diamond teeth.

401
00:31:02,761 --> 00:31:07,900
This is a rocking gang, what they call a lift gang.

402
00:31:08,280 --> 00:31:10,820
And it lifts and hits, lifts and hits.

403
00:31:10,900 --> 00:31:14,040
And the slurry gets trapped in between the segments and in the middle.

404
00:31:14,500 --> 00:31:16,680
Because when it's cutting, the first one will cut.

405
00:31:17,280 --> 00:31:19,780
Then the slurry will get trapped in between the segments.

406
00:31:20,220 --> 00:31:22,580
And then the second one has a cleaner spot to cut.

407
00:31:23,800 --> 00:31:27,360
And it just carries it all out to the end of the blade with water.

408
00:31:27,361 --> 00:31:33,040
it will take these gang saws an eight-hour shift to cut these blocks into slabs

409
00:31:33,041 --> 00:31:40,580
over at indiana limestone company's fully modern mill the job of slabbing stone block from their

410
00:31:40,581 --> 00:31:47,360
quarry is accomplished by gigantic diamond belt saws the blocks come directly from the quarry

411
00:31:47,361 --> 00:31:52,920
looking just like this and the first real operation of fabrication that we do is to slab them

412
00:31:52,921 --> 00:31:55,920
We use these narrow belt saws.

413
00:31:55,921 --> 00:32:01,920
Cutting 14 inches per minute, this saw slices a block into seven slabs in one hour.

414
00:32:01,921 --> 00:32:07,920
It works much like the quarry belt saw, but uses a narrower cutting belt.

415
00:32:07,921 --> 00:32:14,920
The belt travels around in a circle through the two big wheels, hydroplaning across it.

416
00:32:14,921 --> 00:32:21,920
It's about washing the slurry out, allowing the diamond to dig next and dig next, and if the slurry is there, it can't do that.

417
00:32:21,921 --> 00:32:33,919
Once blocks have been slabbed, they're run through an assembly line of 40, 48, and 64-inch circular saws, tipped with diamond cutting teeth and running at a surface

418
00:32:33,920 --> 00:32:36,260
speed of 10,000 feet per minute.

419
00:32:37,920 --> 00:32:43,840
The stone is cut to last, and for workers in this industry, longevity is also key.

420
00:32:45,180 --> 00:32:50,940
Bill Byers and Eddie Brooking have been brazing diamond teeth to saws for a combined 84 years.

421
00:32:51,920 --> 00:32:57,460
Well, we have a lot of mills in this area, but we ship all over the United States.

422
00:32:57,760 --> 00:33:00,780
Not only here, it's everywhere, you know, Canada, everywhere.

423
00:33:01,320 --> 00:33:04,640
But this is what they call the home of the limestone.

424
00:33:05,820 --> 00:33:09,120
Limestone capital of the world is what they bill it as.

425
00:33:10,640 --> 00:33:15,060
To cut stone, mill and quarry saws get down and dirty every day.

426
00:33:16,460 --> 00:33:20,340
But these saws did their work awash in blood and human suffering.

427
00:33:20,341 --> 00:33:26,540
at a time when amputation was the best option and undiscovered organisms the worst enemy.

428
00:33:27,980 --> 00:33:30,500
According to the United States Geological Survey,

429
00:33:30,980 --> 00:33:37,421
stone-cutting saws are among the largest users of the over one-quarter billion carats of industrial diamonds produced in the U.S.

430
00:33:37,422 --> 00:33:38,122
each year.

431
00:33:39,240 --> 00:33:41,420
Saws will return on Modern Marvels.

432
00:33:43,500 --> 00:33:46,700
We now return to Saws on Modern Marvels.

433
00:33:46,701 --> 00:33:52,940
When you look at a saw, what it's designed to cut is not always obvious.

434
00:33:54,200 --> 00:33:57,400
The basic wood saws, most of us recognize them.

435
00:33:58,140 --> 00:34:01,160
Stone saws, a little less common and harder to place.

436
00:34:02,380 --> 00:34:09,920
But this saw, with a surprising amount of power and speed, it must be for something fairly serious.

437
00:34:11,420 --> 00:34:12,520
And it is.

438
00:34:13,060 --> 00:34:16,460
This is the M-Power 6300 oscillating saw.

439
00:34:16,700 --> 00:34:18,420
designed to cut bone.

440
00:34:19,780 --> 00:34:24,380
A surgeon turns to this saw for large bone surgeries like total hip and knee replacement

441
00:34:24,381 --> 00:34:29,920
or for more delicate procedures like anterior cruciate ligament or ACL repair.

442
00:34:30,880 --> 00:34:37,820
In several sporting activities, the anterior cruciate or the front ligament tends to be torn and can get torn.

443
00:34:38,300 --> 00:34:44,500
What we need to do, surgically speaking, is to replace that ligament with a separate tendon that we harvest from the patient.

444
00:34:46,700 --> 00:34:54,640
The replacement tendon will be anchored by two 10 by 25 millimeter bone grafts harvested from the knee by the M-Power 6300.

445
00:34:55,480 --> 00:35:05,943
This cordless $17,000 steel, aluminum, and plastic saw system can accommodate over 100 different types of blades tipped with serrated teeth and covered in a hardened

446
00:35:05,944 --> 00:35:09,240
chrome coating to reduce friction when cutting bone.

447
00:35:09,241 --> 00:35:17,560
The saw's variable speed blade, oscillating from a dead start to 14,000 cycles per minute,

448
00:35:17,561 --> 00:35:19,560
offers the surgeon increased control.

449
00:35:19,561 --> 00:35:26,240
I could start it up slowly just to score the bone without much vibration or jump until

450
00:35:26,241 --> 00:35:32,120
I get it started, and then I could go ahead and go for broke.

451
00:35:32,121 --> 00:35:37,120
The combination of a thin profile yet stiff blade allows the surgeon to plunge the saw's

452
00:35:37,121 --> 00:35:39,200
tip directly into bone.

453
00:35:39,201 --> 00:35:42,820
The saw blade has depth marks on it, and I can watch my depth marks as I cut.

454
00:35:46,420 --> 00:35:53,800
In an actual surgery, these bone grafts would be screwed in place to anchor the new tendon replacement for the torn anterior cruciate ligament.

455
00:35:56,120 --> 00:35:59,700
Ultimately, the greatest challenge for surgical saws comes after surgery,

456
00:36:00,140 --> 00:36:05,960
when they're cleaned and then run through an autoclave, a device that uses steam to sterilize equipment.

457
00:36:05,961 --> 00:36:10,960
The autoclave is run at about 272 degrees Fahrenheit.

458
00:36:10,961 --> 00:36:15,960
So, looking at that, you have to have electronic components that can withstand that type of heat,

459
00:36:15,961 --> 00:36:19,960
go through there hundreds of cycles a year for each handpiece,

460
00:36:19,961 --> 00:36:22,960
and then still focusing on getting it to work each time.

461
00:36:24,960 --> 00:36:27,960
For all but the most recent history of medical saws,

462
00:36:27,961 --> 00:36:32,960
germs were unknown, and sterilization was not practiced.

463
00:36:32,961 --> 00:36:38,460
Early physicians knew that cutting off a limb was really the only way to save a patient's life.

464
00:36:38,640 --> 00:36:42,620
There were no antibiotics. There was nothing else you can do to avoid sepsis.

465
00:36:43,040 --> 00:36:48,620
So even going back to Greek and Roman times, amputation was an operation that was carried out.

466
00:36:50,140 --> 00:36:55,760
A surgeon would have given this 1730s German amputation saw only a cursory cleaning.

467
00:36:55,761 --> 00:37:00,000
Duralization came in at the end of the 19th century.

468
00:37:00,520 --> 00:37:05,120
So essentially, they would take this saw from patient to patient without cleaning it,

469
00:37:05,180 --> 00:37:08,920
and that was one of the reasons why infection killed far more people than the surgery itself.

470
00:37:09,760 --> 00:37:11,680
The friend of the surgeon in those days was speed.

471
00:37:11,780 --> 00:37:15,440
The faster you could get the limb off, the better, because patients didn't tolerate it very well.

472
00:37:15,980 --> 00:37:19,960
And it would be used to saw back and forth on a patient's arm or leg

473
00:37:19,961 --> 00:37:22,720
while two assistants or more were holding the patient down.

474
00:37:22,721 --> 00:37:32,100
Though this practice must have been brutal, it's even harder to imagine the use of the earliest medical saws, prehistoric stone instruments.

475
00:37:32,400 --> 00:37:42,253
The earliest civilizations, thousands of years ago, used saw-like instruments made from flint stones, actually, to cut into bones and even do potentially brain surgery

476
00:37:42,254 --> 00:37:43,020
in the skull.

477
00:37:44,020 --> 00:37:51,760
Trepanation, sawing or boring holes in the skull, is one of the oldest surgeries, dating back as far as 6,500 years.

478
00:37:52,640 --> 00:37:55,040
The practice was widespread among the Inca and Maya,

479
00:37:55,880 --> 00:37:59,460
and was accomplished by abrasion, cutting, and drilling techniques.

480
00:38:00,680 --> 00:38:05,920
It speculated that these cultures trepaned to treat head trauma suffered in battle or accidents,

481
00:38:06,500 --> 00:38:11,700
to relieve headaches, for ritualistic reasons, and even perhaps to release evil spirits.

482
00:38:14,000 --> 00:38:17,640
Today, trepanation is still used in some neurosurgical procedures.

483
00:38:17,641 --> 00:38:23,920
This trefine set was handcrafted in Philadelphia in the 1840s

484
00:38:23,921 --> 00:38:27,480
and would have been used to treat hematomas, or bleeding on the brain.

485
00:38:29,600 --> 00:38:33,400
By cutting a hole in the skull, the surgeon allowed blood to exit,

486
00:38:34,040 --> 00:38:35,860
thus relieving pressure on the patient's brain.

487
00:38:37,060 --> 00:38:40,800
The way that the trefine would work is there was a central pin,

488
00:38:41,300 --> 00:38:46,340
and the pin would slide in and out to direct exactly where the cuts will go.

489
00:38:47,340 --> 00:38:54,680
The trephine would be turned, and the cut made would be circular to whatever depth was indicated.

490
00:38:55,000 --> 00:39:00,620
And then the skull flap was then lifted with an elevator, which is this instrument right here.

491
00:39:03,820 --> 00:39:05,460
Sawbones. Carpenter.

492
00:39:06,220 --> 00:39:09,660
Though it's unclear when these slang terms for doctors entered the lexicon,

493
00:39:10,300 --> 00:39:16,060
they derive from a surgical practice that, though ancient, came into its own as a result of modern warfare.

494
00:39:16,340 --> 00:39:22,540
Most Civil War amputation sets have multiple thin blades because they had to be traded out quite frequently.

495
00:39:23,220 --> 00:39:26,920
There were over 30,000 amputations in Union forces alone.

496
00:39:26,921 --> 00:39:35,340
Everything from fingers, which the survival rate was quite good for, up to mid-thigh amputations where the survival rate was less than 50%,

497
00:39:35,341 --> 00:39:42,400
both from bleeding and trauma of surgery and also infection, which obviously set in very commonly prior to the age of antibiotics.

498
00:39:42,401 --> 00:39:50,200
This early saw, designed by German instrument maker Bernard Heine in the 1830s, never really caught on.

499
00:39:50,760 --> 00:39:52,140
But it was notable nonetheless.

500
00:39:53,040 --> 00:39:55,740
It is the first mechanical chainsaw.

501
00:39:56,080 --> 00:39:58,700
And it, as you can see, is a bit unwieldy to use.

502
00:39:58,780 --> 00:39:59,540
It's quite heavy.

503
00:40:00,040 --> 00:40:04,200
It has to be directed to a very specific point for the very small teeth.

504
00:40:04,680 --> 00:40:10,600
And it was so expensive to make and to maintain that I just don't think it survived very well.

505
00:40:10,601 --> 00:40:16,840
Medical saws came in a variety of shapes and sizes, but they all had the same purpose.

506
00:40:17,400 --> 00:40:19,420
To heal. To save lives.

507
00:40:21,500 --> 00:40:28,120
This familiar saw has been put to a surprising use, and it comes through in the clutch when every second counts.

508
00:40:30,440 --> 00:40:34,080
More than 50,000 amputations were performed during the Civil War.

509
00:40:34,720 --> 00:40:39,640
Either opium or chloroform were used as general anesthetics in 95% of surgeries.

510
00:40:39,641 --> 00:40:42,640
Saws will return on Modern Marvels.

511
00:40:44,640 --> 00:40:47,640
We now return to Saws on Modern Marvels.

512
00:40:50,640 --> 00:40:57,640
When it comes to life and death, you need a chainsaw that can cut through walls and roofs in any rescue situation.

513
00:40:58,640 --> 00:41:01,640
Earthquake. Hurricane. Fire.

514
00:41:03,640 --> 00:41:08,640
Smoke pours from a burning building. A firefighter is trapped inside.

515
00:41:09,640 --> 00:41:13,360
Each passing moment cuts into his chances of survival.

516
00:41:14,040 --> 00:41:14,840
We're making an entry.

517
00:41:19,100 --> 00:41:21,240
Though this is just a training exercise,

518
00:41:21,540 --> 00:41:24,140
it highlights the importance of one piece of equipment,

519
00:41:25,120 --> 00:41:26,880
the steel magnum rescue saw.

520
00:41:28,240 --> 00:41:30,560
That saw, when it encounters something behind there,

521
00:41:31,060 --> 00:41:34,460
conduit, nails, two-by-fours, four-by-fours,

522
00:41:34,640 --> 00:41:36,560
it cuts right through that with no problem at all.

523
00:41:39,640 --> 00:41:44,740
We went in there with a quick extraction and got him out here to safety.

524
00:41:45,460 --> 00:41:46,160
All was good.

525
00:41:47,720 --> 00:41:52,300
Designed exclusively for rescues, this saw can make repeated cuts through wood and metal.

526
00:41:52,780 --> 00:41:54,460
This durability is what makes it special.

527
00:41:56,520 --> 00:41:59,940
Though firefighters have used chainsaws since the 1950s,

528
00:42:00,320 --> 00:42:03,220
this specialized type of power saw evolved slowly.

529
00:42:05,300 --> 00:42:08,480
Chainsaw chains were prone to breaking when they encountered extreme heat

530
00:42:08,481 --> 00:42:10,880
and metals embedded in walls and roofs.

531
00:42:12,200 --> 00:42:17,580
So for decades, firefighters relied mostly on axes to chop out access and ventilation holes.

532
00:42:19,240 --> 00:42:22,620
But this process could eat up valuable time during a rescue effort.

533
00:42:24,820 --> 00:42:27,640
In 1992, faced with these obstacles,

534
00:42:28,400 --> 00:42:32,720
the Virginia Beach Fire Department sought the help of the world's largest chainsaw manufacturer,

535
00:42:33,660 --> 00:42:34,360
steel.

536
00:42:34,361 --> 00:42:39,780
Steele's engineers designed a more durable saw that could withstand the extreme heat

537
00:42:39,781 --> 00:42:42,100
firefighters face when cutting ventilation holes.

538
00:42:44,320 --> 00:42:48,780
The best standard chains may only have a portion of the cutter tipped in carbide,

539
00:42:50,040 --> 00:42:53,900
whereas the cutting edge on the RDR, or Rapid Duro Rescue Chain,

540
00:42:54,280 --> 00:42:59,080
is entirely covered in carbide, which gives the teeth greater impact resistance.

541
00:42:59,081 --> 00:43:07,820
At the firefighters' request, saw designers also made modifications to the body of the chainsaw.

542
00:43:09,560 --> 00:43:12,120
Our structural firefighting gloves are big and they're thick.

543
00:43:12,380 --> 00:43:16,780
It's very difficult to grip the old starter handle, so they developed this force right here.

544
00:43:16,860 --> 00:43:19,140
It's very easy to grab with your gloved hand.

545
00:43:19,600 --> 00:43:22,300
Another thing that they did was this wrap around handle right here.

546
00:43:22,360 --> 00:43:24,600
This gives us the ability to use it right or left.

547
00:43:24,601 --> 00:43:31,020
Most chainsaws are right-handed saws, so we needed that flexibility up on a roof or in a house or somewhere.

548
00:43:31,140 --> 00:43:32,500
We needed right or left operation.

549
00:43:33,780 --> 00:43:36,500
Another thing that you see right here is this front guard.

550
00:43:36,840 --> 00:43:40,940
Basically what happens is when you're cutting asphalt shingles, roofs that have tar in there,

551
00:43:41,640 --> 00:43:43,960
the tar will strike the exhaust system and stick.

552
00:43:44,560 --> 00:43:47,420
So they develop this guard that you see right here.

553
00:43:47,660 --> 00:43:50,660
It's easy to clean up, protects the saw, and works very well.

554
00:43:50,661 --> 00:43:54,700
Saving time that saves lives.

555
00:43:55,300 --> 00:43:57,860
It's not something that we normally associate with saws.

556
00:43:58,860 --> 00:44:01,700
Mostly, they're sharp, powerful, and dangerous.

557
00:44:02,640 --> 00:44:06,520
And when they're doing their thing, it's often best to keep our distance.

558
00:44:08,000 --> 00:44:09,620
But they do our bidding.

559
00:44:10,680 --> 00:44:12,420
Helping us to shape our environment.

560
00:44:13,100 --> 00:44:13,800
To build.

561
00:44:14,520 --> 00:44:15,220
Destroy.

562
00:44:15,880 --> 00:44:17,280
Even to save lives.

563
00:44:17,281 --> 00:44:22,240
and when push comes to shove, they can even make music.

